What the event-source-mapping stand-in must do to match AWS. AWS treats a batch as fully successful when batchItemFailures is empty or null, or the response is empty or null. It treats the whole batch as failed when:
- the response is invalid JSON;
- an itemIdentifier is empty or null;
- the key name is wrong;
- an ID is not in the batch;
- the function throws.
Without ReportBatchItemFailures, any error makes the whole batch visible again, including messages that succeeded. So the stand-in should:
1. ReceiveMessage with up to 10 messages, requesting all system attributes (ApproximateReceiveCount, which the worker's 2^attempt backoff needs) and all message attributes.
2. Build Lambda-shaped SQS records and POST them to the image's RIE at http://worker:8080/2015-03-31/functions/function/invocations.
3. Apply the rules above and DeleteMessageBatch only the successes.
4. Never touch visibility for failures, so the worker's own ChangeMessageVisibility backoff decides when they come back.

Endpoint wiring: AWS_ENDPOINT_URL (global) and AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_<SERVICE> (e.g. AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_DYNAMODB, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_SQS, AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_SSM) are supported by boto3, the SDK for Go V2 and AWS CLI v2. A service-specific variable overrides the global one. So the same images can point at moto, or send DynamoDB alone to dynamodb-local, with no code changes and nothing set in the deployed Lambdas.
